    "created_at": "2015-09-20T15:28:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-20T15:28:18Z",
    "body": "This functionality is already largely provided by the [File API](https://w3c.github.io/FileAPI/). It seems unlikely that implementers would be interested in going through the work of wiring that up to an attribute, given that this adds no new functionality to the web platform.\n\nThere's also no real reason for these to be mutually exclusive (i.e. no real reason to use an attribute). You could add a function like this to your code:\n\n``` js\nfunction fileInputAsArrayBuffer(el, i) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const file = el.files[i];\n    const fileReader = new FileReader();\n    fileReader.onload = () => resolve(fileReader.result);\n    fileReader.onerror => () => reject(fileReader.error);\n  });\n}\n```\n\nThen, instead of doing `inputEl.files[i]` to get the blob, you would do `fileInputAsArrayBuffer(inputEl, i)` to get a (promise for) an ArrayBuffer. The same for all the other types you mention.\n\nSo, I think this is adequately covered by the combination of HTML + the file API already. What do you think?\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
